      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To estimate the effect of oral midazolam on patient pain and anxiety perception during first-trimester surgical abortion."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Between May and December 2013, we conducted a randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled trial. Patients between 6 0/7 and 10 6/7 weeks of gestation received 10 mg oral midazolam or placebo 30-60 minutes before surgical abortion. All patients received ibuprofen and a paracervical block. We powered the study (power=80%; significance level=.025) to detect a 15-mm difference in our two a priori primary outcomes of pain and anxiety with uterine aspiration on a 100-mm visual analog scale. Secondary outcomes were pain and anxiety at additional time points, memory, satisfaction, side effects, and adverse events."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Demographics were similar between groups (placebo=62, midazolam=62). Compared with those randomized to placebo, patients who received midazolam had significantly less anxiety preoperatively (room entry: 51.4 mm compared with 34.5 mm, P<.001; positioning: 56.6 mm compared with 45.4 mm, P=.02). There was no difference in pain (P=.28) or anxiety (P=.14) during uterine aspiration or at other procedural time points. A significantly greater number of patients in the midazolam group reported partial amnesia (31/61 compared with 16/61, P=.005) and dizziness (30/61 compared with 18/61, P=.03). Controlling for baseline differences, patients who received midazolam reported more postoperative sleepiness (P<.001) and less postoperative nausea (P=.004). There was no difference in overall satisfaction (P=.88)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Although oral midazolam reduces preprocedural anxiety, it does not reduce pain or anxiety with uterine aspiration during first-trimester surgical abortions."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "CLINICAL TRIAL REGISTRATION",
          "content": "ClinicalTrials.gov, www.clinicaltrials.gov, NCT01830881."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "LEVEL OF EVIDENCE",
          "content": "I."
        }
      ]},
